IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `sca-nouns`

-- State Courts Act 1970 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition -- LAYER 1, PART 4 (third part): TRANSFERS OF CIVIL PROCEEDINGS (ss 54A to 54G).
-- A judgment the Act hands to a court ("it appears to the court", "if it thinks fit") is an input stated as a fact: the module does not guess what the court will think.
-- A time "as may be prescribed by Rules of Court" is an input too: Rules of Court are not among the sources.

§ `Section 54A -- from a Magistrate's Court to a District Court`

-- src:1710 | 54A.—(1) Where it appears to a District Court, on the application
-- src:1711 | of a party to any civil proceedings pending in a Magistrate’s Court,
-- src:1712 | that the proceedings, by reason of its involving some important
-- src:1717 | question of law, or being a test case, or for any other sufficient reason,
-- src:1718 | should be tried in the District Court, it may order the proceedings to
-- src:1719 | be transferred to the District Court.
@ref s 54A(1) (src:1710-1719)
GIVEN `the application is by a party to civil proceedings pending in a Magistrate's Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears to a District Court that the proceedings should be tried in the District Court by reason of an important question of law, being a test case, or another sufficient reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54A(1) a District Court may order the proceedings to be transferred to the District Court` MEANS
    IF `the application is by a party to civil proceedings pending in a Magistrate's Court` AND `it appears to a District Court that the proceedings should be tried in the District Court by reason of an important question of law, being a test case, or another sufficient reason`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where it appears to a District Court, on the application of a party to any civil proceedings pending in a Magistrate’s Court, that the proceedings ... should be tried in the District Court, it may order" (a "where" that is not met answers JUST FALSE, ruling of 2026-11-05 (b))
         JUST FALSE

-- src:1721 | (2) An order under subsection (1) may be made on such terms as the
-- src:1722 | court sees fit.
@ref s 54A(2) (src:1721-1722)
GIVEN `it is an order under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54A(2) the order may be made on such terms as the court sees fit` MEANS
    IF `it is an order under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is about "An order under subsection (1)"
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 54B -- from a State Court to the General Division of the High Court`

-- src:1727 | 54B.—(1) Where it appears to the General Division of the High
-- src:1728 | Court, on the application of a party to any civil proceedings pending
-- src:1729 | in a State Court, that the proceedings, by reason of its involving some
-- src:1730 | important question of law, or being a test case, or for any other
-- src:1731 | sufficient reason, should be tried in the General Division of the High
-- src:1732 | Court, it may order the proceedings to be transferred to the General
-- src:1733 | Division of the High Court.
@ref s 54B(1) (src:1727-1733)
GIVEN `the application is by a party to civil proceedings pending in a State Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears to the General Division of the High Court that the proceedings should be tried in the General Division by reason of an important question of law, being a test case, or another sufficient reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54B(1) the General Division of the High Court may order the proceedings to be transferred to it` MEANS
    IF `the application is by a party to civil proceedings pending in a State Court` AND `it appears to the General Division of the High Court that the proceedings should be tried in the General Division by reason of an important question of law, being a test case, or another sufficient reason`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where it appears to the General Division of the High Court, on the application of a party to any civil proceedings pending in a State Court, that the proceedings ... should be tried in the General Division of the High Court, it may order" (a "where" that is not met answers JUST FALSE, ruling of 2026-11-05 (b))
         JUST FALSE

-- src:1737 | (2) An order under subsection (1) may be made on such terms as the
-- src:1738 | court sees fit.
@ref s 54B(2) (src:1737-1738)
GIVEN `it is an order under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54B(2) the order may be made on such terms as the court sees fit` MEANS
    IF `it is an order under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is about "An order under subsection (1)"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:1740 | Explanation.— The intended enforcement overseas of any judgment obtained in
-- src:1741 | the General Division of the High Court, under any enforcement arrangements
-- src:1742 | currently in force, would ordinarily be sufficient reason for transferring the
-- src:1743 | proceedings to the General Division of the High Court.
@ref s 54B Explanation (src:1740-1743)
GIVEN `the intended enforcement overseas of a judgment obtained in the General Division of the High Court, under enforcement arrangements currently in force` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54B Explanation the intended enforcement overseas would ordinarily be sufficient reason for transferring the proceedings to the General Division of the High Court` MEANS
    IF `the intended enforcement overseas of a judgment obtained in the General Division of the High Court, under enforcement arrangements currently in force`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Explanation says what intended enforcement overseas "would ordinarily" amount to and says nothing of any other fact
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 54C -- from the General Division of the High Court to a State Court`

-- src:1750 | 54C.—(1) A party to any civil proceedings pending in the General
-- src:1751 | Division of the High Court may for any sufficient reason at any time
-- src:1752 | apply to the General Division of the High Court for an order that the
-- src:1753 | proceedings be transferred to a State Court.
@ref s 54C(1) (src:1750-1753)
GIVEN `the proceedings are civil proceedings pending in the General Division of the High Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is a sufficient reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54C(1) a party may apply to the General Division of the High Court for an order that the proceedings be transferred to a State Court` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are civil proceedings pending in the General Division of the High Court` AND `there is a sufficient reason`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the grant is for "any sufficient reason" and over "civil proceedings pending in the General Division of the High Court"; the subsection does not say that a party without one may not apply
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:1757 | (2) Subject to subsection (3), the General Division of the High
-- src:1758 | Court may, if it thinks fit, and on such terms as it sees fit, and either on
-- src:1759 | its own motion or on application, order that the proceedings be
-- src:1760 | transferred accordingly despite any other provision of this Act.
@ref s 54C(2) (src:1757-1760)
GIVEN `the General Division of the High Court thinks fit` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it acts on its own motion or on application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54C(2) the General Division of the High Court may order the proceedings to be transferred to a State Court` MEANS
    IF `it acts on its own motion or on application` AND `the General Division of the High Court thinks fit`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Subject to subsection (3)" and "if it thinks fit": the judgment is the General Division's; see s 54C(3) for the bar
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:1767 | (3) An order under subsection (2) may only be made in respect of
-- src:1768 | such proceedings as could have been commenced in the State Court to
-- src:1769 | which the application relates, if the value of the claim had been within
-- src:1770 | the District Court limit or the Magistrate’s Court limit, as the case
-- src:1771 | may be.
@ref s 54C(3) (src:1767-1771)
GIVEN `the proceedings could have been commenced in the State Court to which the application relates, if the value of the claim had been within the District Court limit or the Magistrate's Court limit, as the case may be` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54C(3) an order under subsection (2) may be made in respect of the proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings could have been commenced in the State Court to which the application relates, if the value of the claim had been within the District Court limit or the Magistrate's Court limit, as the case may be`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may only be made in respect of such proceedings as could have been commenced in the State Court"; when the proceedings could have been, the bar is lifted and a lifted bar grants nothing (QA); subsection (2) is the grant
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An order under subsection (2) may only be made in respect of such proceedings as could have been commenced in the State Court to which the application relates, if the value of the claim had been within the District Court limit or the Magistrate’s Court limit"
         JUST FALSE

-- src:1773 | Explanation.— The fact that the proceedings fall within the civil jurisdiction of
-- src:1774 | the State Courts would not, by itself, ordinarily constitute sufficient reason for
-- src:1775 | transferring the proceedings to the State Courts, if enforcement overseas is
-- src:1776 | intended of any judgment obtained in the General Division of the High Court
-- src:1777 | under any enforcement arrangements currently in force.
@ref s 54C Explanation (src:1773-1777)
GIVEN `enforcement overseas of a judgment obtained in the General Division of the High Court under enforcement arrangements currently in force is intended` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54C Explanation the fact that the proceedings fall within the civil jurisdiction of the State Courts is by itself ordinarily sufficient reason for transferring the proceedings to the State Courts` MEANS
    IF `enforcement overseas of a judgment obtained in the General Division of the High Court under enforcement arrangements currently in force is intended`
    THEN -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The fact that the proceedings fall within the civil jurisdiction of the State Courts would not, by itself, ordinarily constitute sufficient reason for transferring the proceedings to the State Courts, if enforcement overseas is intended"
         JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Explanation speaks only of the case where enforcement overseas is intended
    ELSE NOTHING

